course: technology
lesson: 20
title: Buses, trams, lorries and traffic lights
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A busy street is a big shared machine: buses, trams, lorries and people all move at once, and traffic lights and crossings take turns so they don't crash into each other.
explain: Much of it is quietly clever: the bus stop sign knows when your bus is coming from its GPS, the crossing box has a little spinning cone for blind people, and a tap of a card pays your fare by radio.
explain: Big vehicles have big rules: a tram runs on rails and cannot swerve, and a lorry is so long it swings wide on corners and needs a long way to stop.
explain: Picture the ride to school on the top deck: you press the bell, the «bus stopping» sign lights up, the doors beep, and you wait until they are fully open before stepping off.
explain: Wherever you are, the safe habit is the same: cross on the green figure, still look both ways, and never run in front of or behind a bus.
fact: A double-decker bus has its engine at the back and its heavy parts low down, so its centre of weight is low; London buses have been tilt-tested to show they stay up on a steep slope.
fact: On a bus you hold on when it moves and sit down upstairs, and you never run in front of or behind a bus.
fact: Live bus stop signs use the GPS position each bus sends, worked out from satellites in space, and a computer works out when it will arrive; a bus stuck in traffic can still show «due».
fact: Pressing the bell lights a sign at the front for the driver; the driver opens the doors with a button, the doors beep to warn that they are closing, and sensors and rubber edges stop them closing on people.
fact: A tram takes electricity from an overhead wire through a pole on its roof and runs on rails in the street, so it is quiet and cannot swerve out of the way.
fact: Bike wheels can get stuck in tram rails, so cyclists cross them at an angle.
fact: Traffic lights change on a timer or when sensors in the road notice a waiting car, and computers can link them so traffic flows; the first traffic lights, in London in 1868, were gas lamps.
fact: Pressing a crossing button tells the lights someone wants to cross; a beep or a small cone that spins under the box tells blind people when it is safe.
fact: You cross on the green figure only, and you still check that cars have stopped.
fact: A coach is a big bus with luggage space underneath and seat belts, which passengers wear; coach drivers are limited by law in how long they can drive without a break.
fact: Bus lanes are painted lanes kept for buses so they stay on time, and cameras check that cars don't use them; a driver caught on camera in a bus lane can be fined.
fact: Tapping a card or phone on a bus reader pays the fare by a short radio signal; the card has a tiny antenna coil inside.
fact: A lorry's big diesel engine pulls a heavy trailer, and lots of wheels spread the weight; a full lorry can weigh as much as thirty cars.
fact: Lorries need a very long way to stop and swing out wide when they turn, so you never cycle on the inside of a turning lorry.
exercise: Why doesn't a double-decker bus fall over? | Its heavy parts are low down, so its centre of weight is low | Its top deck is filled with air | It has magnets in the tyres | The driver leans the other way
exercise: Where should you never run when a bus is at the stop? | In front of it or behind it | Onto the pavement by the stop | Into the bus shelter out of the rain | Away from the road and the kerb
exercise: How does a live bus stop sign know when the bus is coming? | Each bus sends its GPS position to a computer | The driver phones every stop on the way to tell them | The sign hears the engine | A camera counts the passengers
exercise: Why might the sign say «due» but no bus comes yet? | The bus can still be stuck in traffic | The sign is lying just for fun | The bus is invisible | The bus went the other way round the whole town first
exercise: What happens when you press the bell on a bus? | A sign lights up at the front for the driver | The doors open at once, even while the bus is moving | The bus speeds up | The driver gets a text message
exercise: What stops bus doors closing on people? | Sensors and soft rubber edges | The driver holds them open by hand | The doors are too slow to hurt | A spring that never closes fully
exercise: Where does a tram get its electricity? | From an overhead wire through a pole on its roof | From a petrol tank under the floor | From batteries charged by passengers | From the sun on its windows
exercise: Why can't a tram steer out of the way? | It runs on rails | Its driver isn't allowed to | It has no brakes | It only goes in a circle
exercise: How should a cyclist cross tram rails? | At an angle, so the wheel doesn't get stuck | Straight along the rail, keeping the wheel inside the groove | As fast as possible, no matter how | With eyes closed for balance
exercise: How do traffic lights know a car is waiting? | Sensors in the road notice it | The driver honks the horn twice | A police officer phones them up | They guess from the weather and the time
exercise: What were the first traffic lights in London in 1868? | Gas lamps | Electric LEDs | Flags on poles | Candles in jars
exercise: What is the little cone under the crossing box for? | It spins to tell blind people it is safe to cross | It holds the box onto the pole | It is a tiny fan to cool the lights | It counts how many people press
exercise: When the green figure shows, what else do you do? | Still check that cars have stopped | Run across with your eyes shut tight so you go faster | Press the button again | Wait for it to turn red
exercise: Why do coach passengers wear seat belts? | Coaches go fast on long roads and have belts fitted | Belts keep the luggage safe | Belts stop people buying snacks | Belts are only for the driver
exercise: Why do buses get their own lane? | To help them stay on time | So bus drivers can race | So cars can park there | To give the cameras something to film
exercise: How does tapping a card pay for the bus? | A short radio signal talks to the reader through a tiny coil | The reader reads the card's picture | The card sends a letter to the bank | The driver writes it in a book
exercise: Why does a lorry swing out wide when it turns? | It is very long, so its back cuts the corner | The driver wants to show off | Its steering only works one way, so it has to go round in a big loop | The road is too narrow for cars
exercise: A bus has 2 wheels at the front and 4 at the back. How many wheels on 3 buses? | 18 | 6 | 12 | 24
test: my mate says I can cycle up the inside of the lorry while it turns, it's waiting. true? | No, never cycle on the inside of a turning lorry | Yes, if you go fast | Yes, lorries always see bikes in their big mirrors, so you're fine | No, but a scooter is fine
test: shut up, the bus is there, just leg it across in front of it! What's the rule? | Never run in front of or behind a bus | Run, buses always wait for children | Run if you're fast | Only run if the driver waves
test: In a cartoon a tram swerves round a dog on the track. What is true? | A tram runs on rails and cannot swerve | Trams can drive anywhere they like on the road | Trams jump over animals | Trams have steering wheels like cars
test: What would happen if a car drove in the bus lane past the camera? | The camera would record it and the driver could be fined | The camera would stop the car | Nothing, cameras are for show | The bus would push it out of the lane
test: Ella reckons the crossing button does nothing and it's just there to keep you busy. Right? | No, it tells the lights someone is waiting to cross | Yes, the lights never listen, they just change on a timer all day | Yes, it is only decoration | No, it calls the police
test: Which is true about lorries? | They need a very long way to stop | They can stop faster than bikes | They are lighter than a family car | They never have blind spots
test: oi, how come the bus beeps when the doors shut? | To warn people the doors are closing | Because the driver is cross with you | To tell the bus to go faster | To say the bell is broken
test: Grandad says a full lorry weighs as much as two cars. Is he right? | No, as much as about thirty cars | Yes, exactly two cars, give or take | Yes, lorries are light because they're hollow | No, only about as much as one bike
test: Ruby's bike wheel got stuck in a tram rail. What should she do next time? | Cross the rails at an angle | Ride inside the rail on purpose | Go faster straight along it | Close her eyes and hope
test: The top deck is moving and Tom's standing up to look out. What's the rule? | Sit down upstairs and hold on | Stand at the front window | Run down the stairs to the door | It's fine if the bus is slow
test: A blind lady is at the crossing. How does she know it's safe? | A beep or the spinning cone under the box | She guesses from the wind and the sound of footsteps | The cars tell her | She waits for someone to shout
exam: Identify the means by which a live bus-stop display ascertains the expected time of arrival of a vehicle. | The vehicle transmits its satellite-derived position to a computer | The driver telephones each stop in turn | The display detects the sound of the engine | The display counts waiting passengers
exam: It is given that a pedal cyclist intends to traverse tram rails. State the manner in which the crossing ought to be effected. | At an angle, so that the wheel is not trapped | Parallel to and within the rail | At maximal speed irrespective of the angle of approach | Whilst dismounted upon the rail itself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a cyclist adjacent to a turning lorry? | The cyclist must not proceed on the inside of the lorry | The cyclist may overtake on the inside at speed | The cyclist may rely on the driver's mirrors | The cyclist should stop directly beside the cab
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the earliest traffic signals installed in London in 1868. | They were illuminated by gas | They were powered by solar panels | They employed light-emitting diodes | They were operated by computer
